.TH NEWGRP 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
newgrp \- start a shell with a different group ID
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B newgrp
.RI [ group ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B newgrp
changes the group ID of the process with
.BR setgid (2)
and then executes a shell.
.PP
.I group
may be a group name from the group database or a numeric group ID.
A name is looked up first; if it is not found and the operand is a
non-negative decimal number, that number is used as the group ID
without checking that such a group exists.
With no operand, the group is set back to the login group from the
password entry of the real user ID.
.PP
.B newgrp
does not check group membership or group passwords and is not
installed set-user-ID.
The change therefore succeeds only when
.BR setgid (2)
permits it: always for the superuser, and for other users only when
the target is their real or saved group ID.
Supplementary groups are not changed.
If
.BR setgid (2)
fails, an error is printed and no shell is started.
.PP
The shell is taken from the
.B SHELL
environment variable, or else from the user's password entry, or
else
.IR /bin/sh .
It is executed with no arguments and with
.I argv[0]
set to its path, so it is not a login shell.
The environment is passed unchanged.
.SH EXIT STATUS
On success
.B newgrp
does not return; the exit status is that of the shell.
.TP
.B 1
Too many operands, an unknown group, no password entry for the
user,
.BR setgid (2)
failed, or the shell could not be executed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SHELL
The shell to execute.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/group
Group database.
.TP
.I /etc/passwd
Password database, for the login group and shell.
.SH EXAMPLE
As the superuser, start a shell with group
.BR wheel :
.PP
.EX
# newgrp wheel
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR groups (1),
.BR id (1),
.BR login (1),
.BR setgid (2),
.BR getgrent_r (3)
